description:
Compiling a nested-tuple binary search tree never finishes (fz2 build killed at 120 s on develop 135b67da3 and on c5a03e198; N is a runtime count, so the hang is in compilation). Program:
def insert(nil, k), do: {k, nil, nil}
def insert({v, l, r}, k) when k < v, do: {v, insert(l, k), r}
def insert({v, l, r}, k), do: {v, l, insert(r, k)}
plus a build/size loop over it (repro: /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-j5n-Workspace-fz/d1d265b3-f8e7-419b-8316-906f89c17e6b/scratchpad/9x7-gc/repro/bst.fz).
Telemetry (15 s, /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-j5n-Workspace-fz/d1d265b3-f8e7-419b-8316-906f89c17e6b/scratchpad/9x7-gc/repro/bst-tl.jsonl): AnalyzeActivation is 71 of 216 applied completions; one 13.2 s gap sits inside a single analysis. The canonical string of the tree's inferred type grows ~2.7x per analysis: 22, 82, 242, 682, 1882, 5162, 14122, 38602, 105482 characters. Each analysis unrolls one more {int, :nil | {int, :nil | ...}} level and none widens to a recursive type, so the fixpoint never converges.
Acceptance: the program builds and runs on all three paths with a pinned, small number of AnalyzeActivation completions.
